        "path": ".claude/CLAUDE.md",
        "text": "# Paper-writing norms (read before you start; re-read the checklist before you finish)\n\nYou write a full conference paper from supplied material only (overview, figures, tables, bibliography, template). You run no experiments and have no internet. The goal is a paper a reviewer would call well written, not a paper that merely passes format checks.\n\n## Process\n1. Read ALL supplied material first: the overview, every table file, every figure (look at the images), the template/style files, and the WHOLE bibliography: print and read every entry (do not just grep for names you already guess); keyword searches alone only find what you already know.\n2. Before writing, make a short plan in a scratch file outside submission/: the paper's one-sentence claim, 3-4 contributions, the section outline, which figure/table supports which claim, and a map of bibliography keys to the topics/sentences where they belong.\n3. Write the LaTeX, compile it (run pdflatex, bibtex, pdflatex, pdflatex), read the log, fix every error, undefined reference/citation and overfull box that is visible, and look at the pages you produced.\n4. Do a real revision pass: re-read the compiled PDF and the .tex section by section against the self-check below, edit, recompile. A first draft is never the final paper. Only then finish.\n\n## Structure and prose\n- Abstract: problem, why it is hard, the idea, the headline numbers (copied exactly from the tables), one sentence of significance. No citations needed.\n- Introduction: motivate with the concrete failure of existing work, state the idea, list contributions that are each verifiable in the paper, and point to the main result. End with a roadmap only if it is short.\n- Related work: organise by theme (3-5 paragraphs, each with a topic sentence), say what each line of work does, and end each paragraph by stating how this paper differs. It should be a real section of about a page or more, not a list.\n- Method: define notation once, give every loss/equation with the meaning of each term, say why each component exists, and refer to the architecture figure in the text.\n- Experiments: setup (data, baselines, metrics, protocol) before results; every table and figure is referenced in the text, and the text states what the reader should conclude from it (the comparison, the size of the gap, the cause). Include ablations and analysis supplied in the material; interpret them, do not just restate the numbers.\n- Limitations: a real top-level \\section{Limitations} when the task lists it as a section.\n- Conclusion: be candid about what the material does not establish; no new claims.\n- Write in specific, plain academic prose. No filler (\"in recent years, X has attracted great attention\"), no unexplained superlatives, no repeated sentences across sections.\n\n## Truthfulness\n- Every number, dataset name, method name and claim must come from the supplied material. Never invent results, hyperparameters, seeds, variances or details that the material does not give; when something needed is absent, say what is unreported instead of making it up, or leave it out.\n- Numbers in the abstract/introduction must match the tables exactly.\n\n## Citations (this matters most for quality)\n- Cite a work only where the sentence is about what that work does. Read the entry (title/abstract/venue) first; the claim you attach to it must be true of it.\n- Use the bibliography as your literature: a strong paper has dense, relevant citing, so go through the bibliography topic by topic and cite every entry that genuinely belongs in a sentence of the introduction, related work, method or experiments (baselines, datasets, backbones, tools used, prior findings you build on). Typically this is a large share of the bibliography, because it was assembled for this paper.\n- If the overview names a work that is absent from the bibliography, name it in the text without a \\cite and never invent a key.\n- Entries that do not fit any sentence you can honestly write stay uncited. Never dump keys: no `\\cite{a,b,c,d,e,f,...}` bundles of more than 4 keys, no \"see also\" lists, no appended citation paragraph, and no citing for the sake of coverage. A bundle is acceptable only when the sentence makes a claim that every key in it supports.\n- Prefer attaching a citation to a specific statement (\"X [cite] first showed that ...; Y [cite] extends this to ...\"), which is both more useful to the reader and checkable.\n- Use only keys that exist in the supplied .bib file; use the right command (\\citet for subject, \\citep for parenthetical, or \\cite if the style requires).\n\n## Format\n- Use the supplied template and style files exactly; do not alter margins or fonts. Target the page length the task states (the main text should fill it; do not pad with empty space or repeated content, and do not exceed it).\n- Sections required by the task must exist as real \\section commands. Figures and tables must come from the supplied files with informative captions that stand alone, and are referenced with \\ref.\n- Final files go exactly where the task says; manuscript.pdf must be compiled from the final manuscript.tex.\n\n## Self-check before finishing\n- [ ] Every supplied figure and table is included and discussed in the text.\n- [ ] Each section required by the task exists and is substantive.\n- [ ] No invented numbers or facts; abstract numbers equal table numbers.\n- [ ] Every \\cite key exists in the .bib; no \"??\" or undefined references in the PDF; no TODO text.\n- [ ] Each citation sits in a sentence that is about that work. No key bundles of more than 4. Skim the cite list: any entry I cited only to cite it should be removed or given a real sentence.\n- [ ] Related work is thematic and says how this paper differs.\n- [ ] Page count meets the task's target; the PDF was recompiled after the last edit.\n\n## Results prose and length (added)\n- For each results table or figure, write three things: what is compared, how large the difference is (with the numbers from the table, once), and the cause or reading only if the overview supports it; if the overview gives no cause, say what is not reported instead of guessing. Do not put the same numbers in the prose and a second time in another paragraph.\n- Length is measured, not guessed: after the last compile run `pdfinfo manuscript.pdf | grep Pages` and compare it with the page target the task states. Under the target: add substance (an analysis the material supports, a fuller related work, a stated limitation), never filler. Over the target: cut the weakest text. Recompile and measure again after any change.\n\n\n# Internal-review norms\n\nYou review the paper the writer delivered, as a careful conference reviewer who also checks the files. Do not approve on a quick skim; approve only after doing every check below yourself, and otherwise send the writer a short, concrete list of fixes (location + what to change).\n\nChecks:\n1. Compile-level: manuscript.pdf exists, matches the current manuscript.tex, has the page count the task asks for, has no \"??\", undefined citations or TODOs, and every supplied figure/table is present and referenced. Open and look at several pages.\n2. Truth: compare the abstract, introduction and results text with the supplied overview and tables; flag any number, claim or detail not in the supplied material (invented hyperparameters, seeds, results).\n3. Citations: sample at least 10 \\cite uses (and all multi-key ones) and check the cited work (title in the .bib) actually fits the sentence. Flag key dumps, bundles of more than 4 keys, and citations attached to claims the work does not make. Also check that obviously relevant bibliography entries (baselines, datasets, backbones, closely related work) are not left uncited when a sentence could honestly cite them; ask the writer to add them in context, not as a list.\n4. Writing: related work is thematic and contrasts with this paper; method defines its symbols; the experiments text interprets tables rather than repeating them; no filler or repeated paragraphs; limitations are candid.\nRecord what you checked and what you asked to change in your review record. Approve only when the checks pass.\n\nAdded check: run `pdfinfo manuscript.pdf | grep Pages` yourself and compare with the task's page target. A page count off the target (over, or more than a page under) is a finding, not a note: send it back with the section to extend or cut. Also check that results text gives, per table, what is compared, the gap, and a cause only when the supplied material states one.\n"
       }
